<script>
import { url } from '@abide/abide'
import Doc from '#src/docs/Doc.abide'
import Code from '#src/docs/Code.abide'
import Example from '#src/docs/Example.abide'
import Section from '#src/docs/Section.abide'
import Invoice from './Invoice.abide'
import invoiceSource from './Invoice.abide' with { type: 'text' }
</script>

<Doc>
    <Code language="abide" caption="src/invoices/page.abide — excerpt" code={`<a href={url('/invoices/[id]', { id: invoice.id })}>{invoice.number}</a>`} />

    <Section title="The literal decides the arguments">
        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>The literal carries</th><th><code>url</code> takes</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td>a required segment</td><td>a params argument, required</td></tr>
                <tr><td>only optional or rest segments</td><td>a params argument, optional</td></tr>
                <tr><td>no segments at all</td><td>no params argument</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <p>That is one rule read off the pattern rather than a signature you keep in step with it. A
        required segment maps to a required key, an optional one to an optional key — and leaving an
        optional key out <strong>omits the segment</strong>, rather than producing an address with a
        hole in it. A rest segment takes an array as well as a scalar.</p>

        <p>The <code>Query</code> argument is <strong>last</strong>, always. There is one place a
        query string can go, so no call site has to decide.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="The types are the pattern, read by the compiler">
        <table>
            <thead><tr><th></th><th></th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>ParamsOf&lt;P&gt;</code></td><td>the params object a literal <code>P</code> requires</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>HasSegments&lt;P&gt;</code></td><td>whether <code>P</code> has any dynamic segment at all</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>HasParams&lt;P&gt;</code></td><td>whether <code>P</code> requires a params argument</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>RequiredNames&lt;P&gt;</code></td><td>the <code>[name]</code> segments</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>OptionalNames&lt;P&gt;</code></td><td>the <code>[[name]]</code> segments</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>RestNames&lt;P&gt;</code></td><td>the <code>[...name]</code> segments</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <p>None of these is something an app writes. They are how the signature above is derived
        from a string literal, and they are documented because a compile error mentions them — an
        error naming <code>RequiredNames</code> is telling you which segment you left out.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/pages/add-a-page')}>Routes</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="A link names a page the app has">
        <p><code>url</code>'s literal is a <code>RouteLiteral</code>: one of the patterns the app
        files a page under. <code>abide check</code> declares them into <code>Routes</code>, so a
        literal no page is filed under is refused where it is written (30.26) — the stale
        <code>/invoices/[id]</code> left behind when the route grew a <code>[[tab]]</code> is an
        error, not an address the server answers from somewhere else. A plain <code>tsc</code>
        declares none, and then any literal is taken.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="`navigate` goes there without reloading">
        <Example
            id="navigate-query"
            title="`navigate(url)` — a new address, the same page"
            summary="Three invoices on one account, each with its own address. Type a note, then open another invoice — the address moves and the note is still there."
            files={{
                'Invoice.abide': invoiceSource,
            }}
        >
            <Invoice />
        </Example>

        <p><code>navigate</code> takes the address the page decided on — here a query on the page
        already shown, which moves <code>route.url</code> and leaves the page mounted (30.23).
        Another page is the same call,
        <code>{'navigate(url(\'/invoices/[id]\', { id: \'4310\' }))'}</code>, and mounts that page
        in place.</p>

        <p><code>replace: true</code> writes over the history entry rather than adding one, so Back
        leaves the page rather than stepping through every invoice opened on it.</p>

        <p>It navigates and <strong>does not reload the document</strong>, so the layouts stay, the
        values already loaded stay loaded, and the head's keyed contributions are replaced rather
        than rebuilt. An ordinary <code>&lt;a href&gt;</code> does the same thing —
        <code>navigate</code> is for the case where the decision is made in code rather than by a
        click.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/pages/give-pages-the-same-chrome')}>Layouts</a> ·
        <a href={url('/pages/animate-from-one-page-to-the-next')}>View transitions</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="`route.url` and `route.navigating`">
        <p><code>route.url</code> is where you are. <code>route.navigating</code> is whether a
        navigation is in flight, which is what a progress bar reads and what a link disables itself
        on.</p>

        <p>Both are ambient reads like any other, so a layout showing a loading bar needs nothing
        passed to it and no router object in scope.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/values/show-a-value-that-isnt-there-yet')}>Loading states</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Every address resolves against the mount">
        <p><code>url</code> produces a <strong>mount-relative</strong> address, resolved at read
        time. That is what lets the same build serve at the root and under <code>/docs</code> — and
        it is the reason to reach for <code>url</code> rather than write the string, even for an
        address that will obviously never move.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/pages/serve-the-app-under-a-sub-path')}>Sub-path mounting</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Next">
        <ul>
            <li><a href={url('/pages/add-a-page')}>Routes</a> — the literals these are built
            from</li>
            <li><a href={url('/data/find-the-url-a-handler-answers-on')}>Handler URLs</a> — the
            same job for a handler</li>
            <li><a href={url('/pages/animate-from-one-page-to-the-next')}>View transitions</a> —
            what a navigation can look like</li>
        </ul>
    </Section>
</Doc>
